=0 \\\\ ⟨-3,2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+1,\\,z+1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-80768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-3,2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+1,\\,z+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨-3,2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y+2,\\,z-2⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨2,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+1,\\,z+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+1,\\,z+1⟩=0"] ["math" "⟨3,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+1,\\,z+1⟩=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-3,2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+1,\\,z+1⟩=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-yz-xz$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,3,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(2,3,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy-yz-xz)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(y-z)\\text{ }i+(x-z)\\text{ }j+(-y-x)k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,3,4) & =(3-4)\\text{ }i+(2-4)\\text{ }j+(-3-2)k \\\\  & =-1\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(2,3,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,-2,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-3,\\,z-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)-2(y-3)-5(z-4) & =0 \\\\ -x-2y-5z+28 & =0 \\\\ x+2y+5z-28 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-80767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "8x+2y+5z+7=0"] ["math" "8x+y+z-23=0"] ["math" "x+2y+5z-28=0"] ["math" "x+2y+7z-36=0"] ["math" "x+y+z-9=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2y+5z-28=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-37602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz)=3.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e,{e}^{2},-e),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-37602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{e})}{(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}=-6e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-37602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-37602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-37602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6e"] ["math" "-e"] ["math" "3{e}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{e}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-37602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface $z{x}^{3}=-1+x{y}^{2}.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,-2,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z{x}^{3} & =-1+x{y}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-1.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-2,3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}z-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i-2xy\\text{ }j+{x}^{3}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-2,3) & =(3(1)^{2}(3)-(-2)^{2})\\text{ }i-2(1)(-2)\\text{ }j+(1)^{3}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5\\,i+4\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-2),\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+2,\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5(x-1)+4(y+2)+(z-3) & =0 \\\\ 5x+4y+z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-80783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x+y+z=0"] ["math" "2x-4y+2z=16"] ["math" "3x+y+z=4"] ["math" "5x+4y+z=0"] ["math" "x+2y+z=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-80783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5x+4y+z=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-36765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}=-xyz.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(4,2,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-36765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =-xyz \\\\ {x}^{2}+xyz & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(4,2,-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(4,2,-2) & =(2(4)+2(-2))\\text{ }i+4(-2)\\text{ }j+4(2)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i-8j+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(4,2,-2)\\cdot ⟨x-4,\\,y-2,\\,z+2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,-8,8⟩\\cdot ⟨x-4,\\,y-2,\\,z+2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-4)+(-8)(y-2)+8(z+2) & =0 \\\\ 4x-8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ x-2y+2z+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-36765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-36765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-36765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-y+z-4=0"] ["math" "x+2y-2z-12=0"] ["math" "x-2y+2z+4=0"] ["math" "x-2y+2z-8=0"] ["math" "x-4y+z+6=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-36765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2y+2z+4=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface $z{e}^{x}+xy={x}^{2}.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(-1,1,2e).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z{e}^{x}+xy & ={x}^{2} \\\\ z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,1,2e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}+y-2x)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+{e}^{x}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,1,2e) & =((2e){e}^{-1}+1-2(-1))\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+{e}^{-1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5\\,i-j+{e}^{-1}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,{e}^{-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,2e)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2e⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,-1,{e}^{-1}⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2e⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5(x+1)+(-1)(y-1)+{e}^{-1}(z-2e) & =0 \\\\ 5x-y+{e}^{-1}z+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-102718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5x-y+ez+6=0"] ["math" "5x-y+{e}^{-1}z+4=0"] ["math" "5x-{e}^{-1}y-z-4=0"] ["math" "ex-2y+ez-6=0"] ["math" "ex-2y+{e}^{-1}z-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5x-y+{e}^{-1}z+4=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface $xy{z}^{2}=6-z.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{z}^{2} & =6-z \\\\ xy{z}^{2}+z & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+(x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2xyz+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,2) & =(1)(2)^{2}\\text{ }i+(1)(2)^{2}\\text{ }j+(2(1)(1)(2)+1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i+4j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-1)+4(y-1)+5(z-2) & =0 \\\\ 4x+4y+5z-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5129adf3751dcc5fc70f5b0705243122ff442eabcb934251a274e2e55c971553"} {"question" "q-102711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x-y+5z-12=0"] ["math" "4x+3y+z+12=0"] ["math" "4x+4y+5z-18=0"] ["math" "4x-y+2z-10=0"] ["math" "x+4y-3z+15=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "q-102711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x+4y+5z-18=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/state.json" "file_sha256" "b7acd2f87bb66e2eada5b47d7f3ed600fb83ad95d81e64a0081f2a44fcdbffef"} {"question" "e-3368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2,$ find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/example-3368.json" "file_sha256" "d4cd96880254d7be82899a7a8edd6dc76093e8e44714daa9c58efb57c496cdd2"} {"question" "e-3368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1) & =2(2)\\text{ }i+2(-2)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j+2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-4\\,,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-(-2),\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,\\,-4,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)-4(y+2)+2(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4x-4y+2z-18 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y+z-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/example-3368.json" "file_sha256" "d4cd96880254d7be82899a7a8edd6dc76093e8e44714daa9c58efb57c496cdd2"} {"question" "e-6183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the surface $z^3y^2 =x^2-5.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at the point $(-1,2,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/example-6183.json" "file_sha256" "8351bc6169f612780ea0032caae562f14ba5671abe8f2e2d130aa471a32a807c"} {"question" "e-6183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{3}{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = z^3y^2 - x^2,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-5.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(-2x)\\text{ }i+2{z}^{3}y\\text{ }j+3{z}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,2,-1) & =(-2)(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)^{3}(2)\\text{ }j+3(-1)^{2}(2)^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-4j+12\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-2,\\,z-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-4,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-2,\\,z+1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2(x+1)-4(y-2)+12(z+1) & =0 \\\\ 2x-4y+12z+22 & =0 \\\\ x-2y+6z+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070916/example-6183.json" "file_sha256" "8351bc6169f612780ea0032caae562f14ba5671abe8f2e2d130aa471a32a807c"}] "usage" {"e-6183" [] "q-37602" [] "e-3368" []} "basis" 2515 "retractions" [[17592186129550 :question/problem "Consider the surface $\\ln (2x^{3} + yz) = 3$. Given that $n = ⟨a, b, c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e, e^{2},-e)$, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"] [17592186128830 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1)$."] [17592186128830 :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}i + f_{y}j + f_{z}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})k \\\\\n&= 2xi + 2yj + 2zk \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) &= 2(2)i + 2(-2)j + 2(1)k \\\\\n&= 4i - 4j + 2k \\\\\n&= ⟨4,-4, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y - (-2), z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨4,-4, 2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y + 2, z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n4(x - 2) - 4(y + 2) + 2(z - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 4y + 2z - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 2y + z - 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128933 :question/problem "Consider the surface $z^3y^2 =x^2-5$. Find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at the point $(-1,2,-1)$."] [17592186128933 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{3}y^{2} &= x^{2} - 5 \\\\\nz^{3}y^{2} - x^{2} &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = z^3y^2 - x^2\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=-5\n$$\n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})k \\\\\n&= (-2x)i + 2z^{3}yj + 3z^{2}y^{2}k \\\\\n∇f(-1, 2,-1) &= (-2)(-1)i + 2(-1)^{3}(2)j + 3(-1)^{2}(2)^{2}k \\\\\n&= 2i - 4j + 12k \\\\\n&= ⟨2,-4, 12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(-1, 2,-1) \\cdot ⟨x - (-1), y - 2, z - (-1)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨2,-4, 12⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 2, z + 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2(x + 1) - 4(y - 2) + 12(z + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 4y + 12z + 22 &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2y + 6z + 11 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127095 17592186127096 17592186127097 17592186127098 17592186127099] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127094 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127094 :answer-field/id] [17592186127094 :answer-field/key] [17592186127094 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
